<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seokguram</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seokguram is an artificial cave shrine on Mount Toham near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, built in the mid-8th century under the Unified Silla minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-daeseong">Kim Daeseong</a>. Its domed granite chamber holds a great seated Buddha surrounded by reliefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhisattva">bodhisattvas</a> and guardians, a masterpiece of East Asian Buddhist sculpture. It became a World Heritage Site with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulguksa">Bulguksa</a> in 1995.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seokguram.jpg" alt="Seokguram" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: by eimoberg · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korea-Gyeongju-Seokguram-13.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The grotto is said to have been built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-daeseong">Kim Daeseong</a> and was originally called Seokbulsa, the Stone Buddha Temple. Construction began in 751, in the cultural peak of the kingdom of Unified Silla, and the Silla court completed it in 774, shortly after Kim's death. The tradition of carving Buddhist images and stupas into cliff walls and natural caves began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, although the hard granite of the Korean Peninsula is not suited to carving cliff walls, so the grotto is an artificial structure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Seokguram is a hermitage that forms part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulguksa">Bulguksa</a> temple complex, about 3 kilometres from it on the mountain Tohamsan. It rests 750 metres above sea level and overlooks the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">East Sea</a>. An arched entrance leads to a rectangular antechamber, a narrow corridor lined with bas-reliefs, and a main rotunda. The domed ceiling and corridor were built from more than 360 stone slabs, with no mortar, the structure being held by stone rivets, and natural ventilation was used. The rotunda is slightly irregular, its diameter measuring from 6.58 to 6.8 metres.</p><p>The seated Buddha, 3.5 metres tall on a lotus pedestal 1.34 metres high, probably represents Shakyamuni; the position of its hands symbolises enlightenment. Instead of a halo attached to the head, a granite roundel carved with lotus petals on the back wall creates the illusion of one. Around it are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhisattva">bodhisattvas</a>, ten disciples, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> gods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>, and ten further statues in niches. The Four Heavenly Kings guard the corridor, and an Eleven-faced Avalokitesvara, 2.18 metres tall, stands on the back wall. The identity of the main Buddha is still debated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Repairs took place in 1703 and 1758, in the Joseon period, but Confucian-oriented rulers suppressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and the remote grotto was seriously damaged by the turn of the 20th century. Japanese works of 1913 to 1915 almost completely dismantled and reassembled the structure and encased it in concrete, which caused humidity and leaks; a second round followed in 1920 to 1923. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> a major restoration was ordered in the 1960s by President Park Chung Hee. Seokguram was designated a National Treasure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> in 1962, and the interior can now be viewed only through a glass wall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seokguram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bulguksa temple and the <b>Seokguram</b> cave shrine, begun in the mid-eighth century under the minister Kim Daeseong, are among the masterpieces of East Asian Buddhist art and are World Heritage sites.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
